<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#theatre">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Theatre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Theatre is the performance of drama before an audience, and also the buildings designed for it. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> spread Greek urban forms, including the theatre, into regions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/near-east">Near East</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> that had no previous tradition of such civic institutions. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa Shogunate</a> count the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabuki">kabuki</a> theatre tradition among the cultural achievements that flourished under Tokugawa peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/theatre.jpg" alt="Theatre" width="320" height="466" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Lafayette Photo, London · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bernhardt_Hamlet2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
